        "error": "Invalid date range",
        "message": "Please provide creation_start_date and creation_end_date with a 7 days or less than 7 days difference."
    }```
    


## Add On Order API [/v1/partner/{requestID}/add-on-order{?token}]
___

If any patient details are to be added as an additional order to an already created request, this API can be used for the same.

Request BODY expects the new patient details. Token and request id has to match and all validations on the gender, age and number should pass for a success response.


### Add On Order API [POST]

+ Parameters
  + requestID (required, string) - request_id from the response of create order API.
  + token (required,string) - token from the response of create order API.

+ Request
    + Header
        ```
        api_key:<value>
        ```
    
    + Body
    ```json
    {
        "partner_reference_id": "partner order ID",
        "patient_name": "Cari",
        "age": "23",
        "gender": "male",
        "tests": [
            {
                "test_id": "40"
            }
        ],
        "packages": [
            {
                "package_id": "3"
            }
        ],
        "patient_phone": "9071864604",
        "partner_notes": ""
    }
    ```
    
+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body
    ```json
    {
        "order": {
            "id": 2016,
            "partnerReferenceId": "harshitha_testing"
        },
        "status": "Created"
    }
    ```
 
  
## Reschedule Order API [/v1/partner/{requestID}/reschedule{?token}]
___

Requests can be reschedule before emedic starts the task. i.e., before tracking link is available in Get Order Status API.

Expects json body with following 3 keys
- requested_slot_time (Time slot, to be fetched using serviceability API)
- reschedule_reason (Example : Patient wants to Reschedule)
- reschedule_explanation (Example : Paitent found a better alternative with xyz company)

### Reschedule Order API [PATCH]

+ Parameters
  + requestID (required, string) - request_id from the response of create order API.
  + token (required,string) - token from the response of create order API.

+ Request
    + Header
        ```
        api_key:<value>
        ```
    + Body
    ```json
    {
        "requested_slot_time": "YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss",
        "reschedule_reason": "(reason as a string)",
        "reschedule_explanation": "(explanation as string)"
    }
    ```
    
    
+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body
    ```json
    {
    "result": "ok"
}```
    
    
    



## Cancel Order API [/v1/partner/{requestID}/cancel{?token}]
___

Requests can be cancelled before emedic starts the task. i.e., before tracking link is available in Get Order Status API.
Please Note : Cancellation is an asynchronous process in Orange Health's system. This API acknowledges that we have received a request for cancellation and DOES NOT guarantee cancellation.
Please use `order.cancelled` event in webhooks or get order status API to confirm cancellation.
### Cancel Order API [PATCH]

+ Parameters
  + requestID (required, string) - request_id from the response of create order API.
  + token (required,string) - token from the response of create order API.

+ Request
    + Header
        ```
        api_key:<value>
        ```
    
    + Body
    ```json
    {
        "cancellationExplanation": "Patient cancelled",
        "cancellationReason": "Found a better option"
    }
    ```
    
+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body
    ```json
    {
        "status": "Cancellation Request with ID : BLR300100 is in process, please check order status to confirm cancellation"
    }```
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
## Webhooks [/webhooks]
___

#### Introduction:

> This document provides guidelines for integrating webhooks with Orange Health. Webhooks allow real-time communication between the Orange Health platform and external systems, enabling the exchange of data and triggering actions based on events. 
  

In this documentation, you can check the different types of example payloads for all the events by changing the example chosen below.

#### Authentication and Security:

> Orange Health uses your API key to create a hash signature with each payload. This hash signature is passed with each request under the X-OH-Signature header that you need to validate at your end. 
  

#### X-OH-Signature

> The hash signature is calculated using HMAC with SHA256 algorithm; with your `secret key` set as the key and the webhook request body as the message. 
  

You can also validate the webhook signature yourself using a [HMAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash-based_message_authentication_code) as shown below:
Ensure you stringify the request_body with 0 whitespaces before generating the hmac signature
HMAC Hex Dige

``` plaintext
key                = webhook_secret
message            = webhook_body // raw webhook request body
received_signature = webhook_signature
expected_signature = hmac('sha256', message, key)
if expected_signature != received_signature
    throw SecurityError
end
```

Below is a code snippet for the logic using javascript, the same can be done with other languages. Stringify should be done with 0 whitespaces.
```
const crypto = require('crypto');
secret_key = "abcd";
const sampleJson = { "test": "test" };
const key = Buffer.from(secret_key, 'utf-8');
const body = JSON.stringify(sampleJson, null, 0);
console.log(body);
const hmac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key);
hmac.update(body);
const generatedSignature = hmac.digest('hex');
console.log(generatedSignature);
```
In the above code snippet, replace sampleJson with the request body and secret_key with your secret key for testing

#### Idempotency

> There could be scenarios where your endpoint might receive the same webhook event multiple times. This is an expected behaviour based on the webhook design. 
  

To handle duplicate webhook events:

1. You can identify the duplicate webhooks using the x-oh-event-id header. The value for this header is unique per event.
2. Check the value of x-oh-event-id in the webhook request header.
3. Verify if an event with the same header is processed at your end.
4. This x-oh-event-id will be a [uuid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier)
    

#### Order of Webhooks

> Ideally, you should receive a webhook in the order in which the webhook events occur. However, you may not always receive the webhooks in the order. 
  

#### Example - Tasks

For example, in the case of Orange Health flow, you should receive webhooks in the following order:

1. order.created
2. task.confirmed
3. task.assigned
4. task.accepted
5. task.started
6. task.in_progress
7. task.completed
8. test.completed_not_sent
9. test.completed_sent
10. order.completed
    

**The above order may not be followed at all times.** You should configure your webhook URL to not expect delivery of these events in this order and handle such scenarios.

#### States :

Order flow end state is ideally \`order.completed\`. However, if the order is cancelled due to any reason. The end state would be \`order.cancelled\` and \`task.deleted\`

#### Delivery Attempts and Retries:

> Every event that receives a non-2xx response is considered an event delivery failure by Orange Health's system. If there is a delivery failure, we retry the delivery in exponential backoff policy for 24 hours after event creation timestamp. 
  
> A webhook is retried at progressive intervals of time on failure, defined in the exponential backoff policy upto 1 day. After the first day, the webhook is retried once every 24 hours. If the webhooks continue to fail for 7 days, the message is disabled. 
  

## Webhook Payload Structure :

The basic structure of the request body will be as shown below with 3 outer keys : 
- Event tells what the event is corresponding to, it will be in the format of object.verb
- Contains is an array of string that contains all the keys that will be present in the payload object
- Payload has the actual payload with relevant data to process information


+ Attributes
    + event : object.verb
    + contains (array):
        + item1
        + item2
    + payload (object):
        + item1 (object)
        + item2 (object)
        

## Webhook Examples : [POST]
Below are the example webhook payload which will be sent to the registered url as a POST API
Webhooks are sent based on order level at Orange Health. Which means, if 2 or more patients from the same location books test with Orange Health, it will create 2 or more orders under 1 request.
Here, 1 request is created with 1 API call to Create Order API.

For the below example, the following payload has been used to create request with Orange Health

Create Order API Request Payload used
```json
{
    "address": "#2268/J Jane Doe Home, Bangalore",
    "location": {
        "latitude": "13.0240",
        "longitude": "77.6433"
    },
    "primary_patient_name": "John Doe",
    "primary_patient_number": "9999999999",
    "slot_datetime": "2024-01-04T06:00:00",
    "payment_type": "paid_by_group",
    "patient_details": [
        {
            "patient_name": "Jane Doe",
            "patient_phone": "9999999998",
            "age": "21",
            "gender": "Female",
            "partner_reference_id":"P_1",
            "tests": [
                {
                    "test_id": "154"
                }
            ],
            "packages": []
        },
        {
            "patient_name": "John Doe",
            "patient_phone": "9999999999",
            "age": "23",
            "gender": "Male",
            "partner_reference_id":"P_2",
            "tests": [
                {
                    "test_id": "469"
                }
            ],
            "packages": []
        }
    ]
}
```

Response for the above request :
```json
{
    "request_id": "BLR520289",
    "token": "61a4c54b-b907-41bf-bce9-b3102e0285ab",
    "status": "Scheduled",
    "orders": [
        {
            "id": 692963,
            "partnerReferenceId": "P_1"
        },
        {
            "id": 692964,
            "partnerReferenceId": "P_2"
        }
    ]
}
```

Below are the examples for all webhooks that were triggered. As the order was moved from creation to completion.
Since, 2 apis will be triggered (1 for Jane Doe patient and 1 for John Doe patient), we have added both examples for each patient.

For this particular events, the secret key set up by partner is `abcd`, this can be used to cross check headers in each webhook events.

